"MDQ6VXNlcjQ4ODQyNDY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4884246?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe","html_url":"https://github.com/mihe","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mihe/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-03-02T11:46:57Z","updated_at":"2026-03-02T11:46:57Z","body":"Nitpicky perhaps, but saying \"the physics engine in Godot\" might also make people think you're strictly talking about Godot's own physics engine. Jolt advertises itself as deterministic, which has made some people think that Godot now by virtue of that has deterministic 3D physics, which of course can't be the case if Godot itself isn't also explicitly deterministic.\r\n\r\nIt might be better phrasing it something along the lines of \"physics in Godot, regardless of physics engine\" instead.\r\n\r\nBut yes, I agree that this seems better suited for the physics introduction manual.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3983883549/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/3983950925","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/114553#issuecomment-3983950925","issue_url":"https://api.githu